From: David J. Vandenburgh <david@vandenburgh.co>
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: qcom_battmgr: work power_now out from current
 and voltage on SM8350-class firmware

On SM8350-class firmware the driver hands BATT_POWER_NOW to userspace as
it comes. On the Retroid Pocket 6 (SM8550) that property is not the
power: it read 55187189 with the handheld idle, while current_now and
voltage_now read -360360 uA and 4246826 uV, which is 1.53 W, and it
stayed between 53 and 55 million through ten minutes of a game.
Anything that trusts power_now, UPower's energy rate and time remaining
for one, is out by a factor of thirty or more.

Current and voltage are correct on the same firmware, so compute
power_now from them, as a magnitude in microwatts, and stop asking the
firmware for it.

Signed-off-by: David J. Vandenburgh <david@vandenburgh.co>
---
diff --git a/drivers/power/supply/qcom_battmgr.c b/drivers/power/supply/qcom_battmgr.c
index 27c49e8..aa8beb4 100644
--- a/drivers/power/supply/qcom_battmgr.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/qcom_battmgr.c
@@ -15,6 +15,7 @@
 #include <linux/soc/qcom/pdr.h>
 #include <linux/soc/qcom/pmic_glink.h>
 #include <linux/math.h>
+#include <linux/math64.h>
 #include <linux/units.h>
 
 #define BATTMGR_CHEMISTRY_LEN	4
@@ -469,7 +470,25 @@ static int qcom_battmgr_bat_sm8350_update(struct qcom_battmgr *battmgr,
 	prop = sm8350_bat_prop_map[psp];
 
 	mutex_lock(&battmgr->lock);
-	ret = qcom_battmgr_request_property(battmgr, BATTMGR_BAT_PROPERTY_GET, prop, 0);
+	if (psp == POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_POWER_NOW) {
+		/*
+		 * BATT_POWER_NOW is not the power on every firmware: the Retroid
+		 * Pocket 6 answers about 55 W whether it draws 1.5 W idle or
+		 * several watts in a game. Current and voltage are right
+		 * there, so work the power out from them.
+		 */
+		ret = qcom_battmgr_request_property(battmgr, BATTMGR_BAT_PROPERTY_GET,
+						    BATT_CURR_NOW, 0);
+		if (!ret)
+			ret = qcom_battmgr_request_property(battmgr, BATTMGR_BAT_PROPERTY_GET,
+							    BATT_VOLT_NOW, 0);
+		if (!ret)
+			battmgr->status.power_now =
+				div_u64((u64)abs(battmgr->status.current_now) *
+					battmgr->status.voltage_now, 1000000);
+	} else {
+		ret = qcom_battmgr_request_property(battmgr, BATTMGR_BAT_PROPERTY_GET, prop, 0);
+	}
 	mutex_unlock(&battmgr->lock);
 
 	return ret;
